  to the part about the confusion between  social democracy and democratic  socialism and I want to take a look at a  couple of them with you because he says  far better than I could where some of  this confusion is and he argues first of  all that the rise of people calling  themselves socialists isn't surprising  because of the acute failings of our  capitalist system so let's go to the  first quote from the article Krugman  writes this rise was predictable and  predicted here's what happened for  decades the right has tried to shout  down any attempt to sand down some of  the rough edges of capitalism whether  through health guarantees income  supports or anything else by yelling  socialism sooner or later people were  bound to say that if any attempt to make  our system less harsh is socialism well  they're socialists this is a really good  point when the left over the last 10 20  30 years has proposed socially  democratic reforms looking at guarantees  of health care higher minimum wages  reducing income inequality these are not  socialist ideas they are ideas of social  democracy the right has responded by  saying you socialists suggesting that  and what has that led to it has  partially led to people thinking well if  that's socialism then I guess I'm a  socialist and of course the capitalist  system also has led to arise in both  socialist thinking and socially  democratic thinking so so far Krugman is  spot on then we get to the key part and  this is exactly what I've been saying  he writes quote the truth is that there  are hardly any people in the United  States who want the government to seize  the means of production or even the  economy's commanding Heights what they  want is social democracy the kinds of  basic guarantees of health care  protection against poverty etc that  almost every other advanced country  provides this is not a criticism of  socialism I said it at the beginning and  I want to say it again if you are  actually a socialist with peace and love  let's debate socialism versus social  democracy and let's have that  conversation  what Krugman is pointing out is that a  lot of the people saying they're  socialists just aren't socialists  this is not semantics we often talk  about Sweden as a social democracy  Krugman talks in his article about  Denmark which he says his quote arguably  the most social democratic country in  the world tax revenues make up 46  percent of GDP in Denmark they have  a very high labor force participation  rate the right claims to care about that  figure they work shorter hours than in  the United States life satisfaction is  higher than in the United States and yes  GDP per capita is lower in Denmark but  it's not affecting quality of life and  it's not affecting life satisfaction or  educational outcomes or health outcomes  so to go back now to the social  democracy versus democratic social  socialism confusion if you want to argue  as some of you do that in 2018 DSA  democratic socialists of America is a  big tent and welcomes everybody buddy  whether or not they are actually  socialist whether or not they want to  socialize the means of production that's  a practical argument that we can have  nobody benefits from people thinking  that what they want is socialism when in  reality they want guaranteed government  health care and a living wage education  paid through taxes and a stronger social  safety net is Paul Krugman write about  everything all the time  no he actually had some really unfair  criticisms of Bernie back during 2016  but he's right about this and I am going  to implore everybody in our audience if  you get an opportunity in conversation  to clarify the confusion between social  democracy and socialism take that and do  it clarify that confusion because I  think it's important yeah I mean that's  the best path forward especially because  the word socialism has been demonized so  much by the right that there's no really  no coming back from it at this point so  if you're not a socialist don't call  yourself a socialist you're just giving  the right-wing ammunition against you  maybe well there's there's a number of  polls that show that actually  socialism has come back in other words  approval of socialism has skyrocketed  relative to where it's been and I think  part of it is again because of what I  described at the beginning of the story  which is because the right has been  calling social democracy democratic  ideas socialists for so long people are  saying oh well if that socialism than it  is a good thing so I think the opposite  which is socialism has had a resurgence  in popularity I just think a lot of the  people who claim to be socialist aren't  actually and this is third time I'm  saying it it's not an argument for or  against socialism it's an argument that  when we talk about economics we should  know what we're talking about and I'm  Paul Krugman wrote about it send me your  thoughts you can always discuss this on  our subreddit or also you can comment on  the David Pakman show  Instagram yeah behind-the-scenes  pictures and so much more we are david  pakman show on instagram follow us there  today's program brought to you in part  by Blink accom slash Pakman blinkie's is  a great service that i use all the time  what they do is they take thousands of  the most popular non-fiction books  including the newest bestsellers they  condense them into 15-minute audiobooks  that you can listen to and you still get  the most crucial information and  insights from each book and i've  listened to a ton of books on blinka  cently checked out  soccer na mcc's by simon Cooper we do  have after all the World Cup going on  recently we also have been talking about  BS jobs and there is a great new book BS  jobs by David Graeber which I listened  to on blinka they're constantly adding  new books to the platform you will never  run out of fascinating and critically  acclaimed books to enjoy and our  audience can get 